[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on one rule: the subject line matches
([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject), replaced by [0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause)). Subjects say how a reporter felt, not what broke.
Read against each report's tenant and channel, the ticket holds more than one problem. Each has its own
ticket in the repo, and engineering closed #4471 on 5 October with a different outcome for each.

<Callout>
Engineering update, #4471 closed 5 October: `parser` **fixed** (`fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`);
`hint` **won't fix** (booking-v3 form retired). One fix did not cover the whole ticket.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## The problems

**`parser` · the shared parser reads every date month first.** A tenant whose locale writes the day first
(every tenant on file) gets its dates flipped: 7 April comes out as 4 July. This is the bug #4471 names. It is by far the largest
group. **Fixed** by `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.

**`hint` · booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field.** On the day-first tenants that run booking-v3, people who book through the form type what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads
it the tenant's way. The parser fix does not touch this: the dates are wrong when they are typed. Only
`form` reports can be this problem; the same tenants' app and email reports are `parser`. **Won't fix:** the
booking-v3 form is retired, so no new reports of this kind can come in.

**Reports read as a one-day move.** Some reports describe a date a day off, not flipped: a Tuesday invite
for a Monday booking, an invite sent before the booking existed, one meeting that reached Lisbon and Toronto
on different days. The desk keeps them where they are filed, under `parser`, and footnotes them
(`desk-notes.json`). They are counted in the `parser` row above, not as a problem of their own. Now that the
parser fix has shipped, if these tenants still see a day off, time zones are the next place to look.

## Tickets

- [#1](https://github.com/ssztyper/megaDev/issues/1) · `parser` · closed, fixed
- [#2](https://github.com/ssztyper/megaDev/issues/2) · `hint` · closed, won't fix (form retired)

## What went wrong in the merge

- Intake tags come from the reporter's browser, a guess made at the door. They disagree with the cause
  from the tenant's data, so a tag is a hint to read the report, not a reason to merge.
- The export repeats some rows. Count by id, once.
- A few reports carry no dates at all. They stay under the cause their tenant gives.

## Lessons

- Merge on cause, not on subject. The cause comes from the tenant and the channel
  ([decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) replaces 0001; [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe) points
  to it).
- A table that adds up to the ticket is not proof that the ticket is one problem.
- Numbers on this page are computed from the export when the page is built. If the desk's data
  changes, the page changes with it.

